## Proof

Let $\mathfrak{h}_n$ be the Heisenberg Lie algebra of dimension $2n+1$, with basis $\{x_1,\dots,x_n,y_1,\dots,y_n,z\}$ and Lie bracket defined by
$$[x_i,y_i]=z,\qquad [x_i,y_j]=0\;(i\neq j),\qquad [x_i,x_j]=[y_i,y_j]=0,$$
and $z$ central. The center is $Z(\mathfrak{h}_n)=\langle z\rangle$, one-dimensional.

Set $V=\langle x_1,\dots,x_n,y_1,\dots,y_n\rangle$, so $\mathfrak{h}_n = V \oplus \langle z\rangle$. Define the symplectic form $\omega$ on $V$ by $\omega(v,w)\,z = [v,w]$, i.e. $\omega = \sum_{i=1}^n dx_i\wedge dy_i$. This is a non-degenerate skew-symmetric bilinear form on the $2n$-dimensional space $V$.

**Upper bound.** Let $\mathfrak{a}\subseteq\mathfrak{h}_n$ be an abelian Lie subalgebra. Let $\pi:\mathfrak{h}_n\to V$ be the projection along $\langle z\rangle$, and set $W=\pi(\mathfrak{a})\subseteq V$. For any $a,b\in\mathfrak{a}$, write $a=v_a+\alpha_a z$, $b=v_b+\alpha_b z$ with $v_a,v_b\in V$. Since $z$ is central,
$$[a,b]=[v_a,v_b]=\omega(v_a,v_b)\,z.$$
The condition $\mathfrak{a}$ abelian gives $[a,b]=0$ for all $a,b\in\mathfrak{a}$, hence $\omega(v_a,v_b)=0$ for all $v_a,v_b\in W$. Thus $W$ is an **isotropic** subspace of $(V,\omega)$.

For an isotropic subspace $W$ of a symplectic space, $W\subseteq W^\perp$ (where $W^\perp=\{v:\omega(v,w)=0\;\forall w\in W\}$), so $\dim W\le \dim W^\perp = 2n-\dim W$, giving $\dim W\le n$.

Now $\ker(\pi|_{\mathfrak{a}})=\mathfrak{a}\cap\langle z\rangle$ has dimension $0$ or $1$, so
$$\dim\mathfrak{a}=\dim W+\dim(\mathfrak{a}\cap\langle z\rangle)\le n+1.$$

**Achievability.** The subspace $\mathfrak{a}=\langle x_1,\dots,x_n,z\rangle$ is a Lie subalgebra (closed under bracket since all brackets among $x_i$ vanish and $z$ is central) and is abelian, with $\dim\mathfrak{a}=n+1$.

Therefore the maximal possible dimension of an abelian Lie subalgebra of $\mathfrak{h}_n$ is $\boxed{n+1}$.

